WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.758
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.758 --> 00:00:08.567
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Aah!

00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:10.839
Aah!

00:00:10.839 --> 00:00:17.008
It's 2 AM on November 9th 2016.

00:00:17.008 --> 00:00:20.796
I was alone in the middle of the street.

00:00:20.796 --> 00:00:24.089
Shouting at the top of my lungs.

00:00:24.089 --> 00:00:29.896
Donald Trump had just been announced as
the next President of the United States.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:36.226
I was overcome by emotion and
the shouting was helping.

00:00:36.226 --> 00:00:40.580
It was helping release some of that
emotional pressure I had been feeling.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.210
A lot of people thought
a lot of things that day.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.170
Some people were ecstatic.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.950
Finally a president had been elected
that really understood their needs.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.360
And others were really sad.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.520
How could someone who had been so
divisive won?

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:05.110
For me, those feelings that night,
those shouts were all about anger.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.620
I was angry because of the uncertainty I
knew a Trump presidency would bring for

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.120
my own country, Mexico.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:16.690
I've thought a lot about that night.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:22.112
What I did, what I could have done.

00:01:22.112 --> 00:01:24.062
And you know what I would
have done if I could go back?

00:01:25.955 --> 00:01:29.005
I would have gone into a library and
I would have run some regressions.

00:01:29.005 --> 00:01:32.160
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.140
&gt;&gt; Today I'd like to tell you why,

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:38.190
with the hope that maybe by telling you
the way I dealt with those emotions,

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:43.040
it can help you figure out how to deal
with the emotions you might be feeling.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.140
I was born and raised in Mexico.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.830
I grew up in a family that has
always believed that Mexico and

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.310
the US should be closer together.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.570
My dad was the chief architect and
negotiator for

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:56.500
Mexico at
the North American Free Trade Agreement.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:01.110
I came to study college in the US.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:02.191
I worked here after that.

00:02:04.636 --> 00:02:08.070
I am about to graduate from two
Masters degrees here in the US.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:11.350
I came to study and work in the United
States because I hope to live a life that

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:13.240
allows me to bring the two
countries closer together.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:18.540
I love Mexico and I have a profound
admiration for the United States.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.390
For me it's always been
obvious that bringing

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.500
our two countries closer
together is a good thing.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:29.660
And yet Donald Trump seemed to stand for
completely the opposite of that.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.668
I was so angry that night
because I couldn't help but

00:02:33.668 --> 00:02:36.908
feel that he had won precisely because he
had promised to divide our two countries.

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:42.159
He literally promised to build
a wall between our two countries.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:44.980
And that made me angry.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.400
I was desperate to take action.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.850
To do something, but
I didn't know what to do.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:58.550
So instead, I started expressing my
emotions in all kinds of platforms.

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:03.767
WhatsApp, Facebook, emails,
in front of people.

00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:07.750
Anything that I could do
to share how I was feeling.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:08.800
I shared how I was angry.

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:11.840
How I hated that the US
was doing this to us.

00:03:13.907 --> 00:03:15.564
I even said that I hated the US.

00:03:17.757 --> 00:03:22.400
And to be honest all of Mexico
started doing the same.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.274
And suddenly, the discourse in Mexico
towards the United States changed in a way

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:30.865
that it hadn't changed
in over three decades.

00:03:30.865 --> 00:03:34.604
Anti-US feelings became the norm.

00:03:34.604 --> 00:03:41.665
An extreme left wing presidential
candidate who is incredibly

00:03:41.665 --> 00:03:46.290
anti-US is now the frontrunner for
the presidential elections in our country.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:52.380
People started calling for
US companies to be kicked out of Mexico.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:57.540
For tariffs to be imposed on US imports.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:01.286
People started calling for
Mexico to push the US out.

00:04:04.857 --> 00:04:11.816
When I saw that, all that anger
I had been feeling became worry.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:24.671
Suddenly, Despite everything
I had been feeling and

00:04:24.671 --> 00:04:31.019
saying, I realize that pushing the US
out was not the right course of action.

00:04:31.019 --> 00:04:34.324
No, what we needed was finding
ways to keep letting the US in.

00:04:36.916 --> 00:04:40.384
And so I set out to prove that with data.

00:04:40.384 --> 00:04:42.801
I suddenly had something to do.

00:04:42.801 --> 00:04:47.114
It wasn't the most exciting action,
but it was some action.

00:04:47.114 --> 00:04:50.959
It consisted in locking
myself up in a library,

00:04:50.959 --> 00:04:55.008
looking at datasets, and
running regressions.

00:04:55.008 --> 00:05:00.030
We can think of what NAFTA
did in two big things.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:05.110
One was that it lowered US tariffs for
Mexican products.

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:07.109
Those are the tariffs that
Donald Trump has threatened to raise.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.500
The second thing it did is that it
lowered Mexican tariffs for US products.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.424
Now Donald Trump gets to decide what
he wants to do with his tariffs, but

00:05:16.424 --> 00:05:18.779
we as Mexico, we get to decide
what we want to do with ours.

00:05:20.698 --> 00:05:24.040
And so the question for
me was what should we do with our tariffs?

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:26.390
Should we raise them and push the US out?

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.940
Or should we keep them at zero,
and keep letting the US come in.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:34.100
The answer wasn't obvious.

00:05:35.585 --> 00:05:37.070
Spent a lot of days in library.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:41.072
But suddenly, l figured it out.

00:05:41.072 --> 00:05:45.212
I was able to show that Mexico has
grown much more because Mexico lowered

00:05:45.212 --> 00:05:47.730
its tarriffs on the US products.

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:51.369
Then because the US
lowered its tariffs on us.

00:05:51.369 --> 00:05:53.500
That means that free trade
has been good for Mexico.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:59.830
That means that businesses in our country
have grown because we opened up to the US.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.794
That means that we
shouldn't push the US out,

00:06:04.794 --> 00:06:08.329
but rather keep finding
ways to let them in.

00:06:12.468 --> 00:06:15.340
Running those regressions gave me clarity.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:21.590
I suddenly knew what I had to do,
I had to find ways from Mexico and

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:24.870
the US to keep in close to one another.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:26.672
Even if my feelings were
telling me not to do that.

00:06:29.596 --> 00:06:31.550
We are all feeling a lot
of things these days.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:33.630
These are emotional times.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.863
Some of us are facing the most challenging
issues many of us have ever seen.

00:06:41.848 --> 00:06:44.140
And it's hard to overcome
the emotions we're feeling.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:47.380
Sometimes they can paralyze us.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:51.850
And other times it can jump us into action
that we maybe haven't fully thought out.

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:58.190
So today I want to ask you to
complement those emotions and

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:00.690
those feelings with data and analysis.

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:07.470
If you're feeling paralyzed, jump into
the data of whatever it is you care about.

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:10.350
And maybe that analysis will give
you clarity on how to move forward.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:16.685
And if you are already jumping
into action, then maybe pause,

00:07:16.685 --> 00:07:21.106
for a moment, and
make sure by looking at that data,

00:07:21.106 --> 00:07:24.336
that what you are doing is going to help.

00:07:24.336 --> 00:07:29.090
If you are marching and protesting,
keep marching and protesting.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:30.957
But make sure you have
the data to back that up.

00:07:34.906 --> 00:07:38.757
When I started preparing to give
this talk a couple months ago,

00:07:38.757 --> 00:07:42.972
my intent was to argue that the way
we can counter the inflammatory and

00:07:42.972 --> 00:07:46.606
outrageous statements made by
someone like Donald Trump,

00:07:46.606 --> 00:07:50.405
is by being equally outrageous and
inflammatory ourselves.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:56.960
And while that might feel good for
an instant, it's not going to do anything.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:05.748
No, today more than ever, we need to
focus on facts, on data, on analysis.

00:08:10.249 --> 00:08:17.216
So next time that you're feeling like you
want to Aah!, go run some regressions.

00:08:17.216 --> 00:08:17.986
Thank you.

00:08:17.986 --> 00:08:19.611
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:19.611 --> 00:08:23.779
[MUSIC]

